[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked **known**: one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it
say otherwise. They were merged on subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and "wrong day on the invite" is what every date bug looks like from the outside. Read by cause, the
reports hold separate problems, and only one of them is the parser fix that is queued.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix will not close #4471. Each problem below has its own ticket, with how many reports it covers.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

Counts are built from `reports-4471.json` at render time with the desk's own view (`causes.ts`, as exported):
one report per id (the export repeats some rows), each counted once, under its cause by tenant. A report
belongs to a cause by its tenant and the way it came in, not by the intake tag: intake guesses from the
reporter's browser, and it is wrong in both directions. The one-day moves stay counted under `parser`,
where the desk files them, and are footnoted there.

### `parser`: day and month swapped by the shared parser

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so a booking
on D/M lands on M/D. In every one of these reports that names both dates, the second is the first with day and month
swapped. The swapped day is always 12 or lower, so the wrong date is still a valid date and nothing errors.

### `hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field and then reads the date the tenant's way, day first.
People type what the hint says. The swap happens in the form, so the parser fix does not touch it.
The #4471 page already flags this row as "not this ticket", but it had no ticket of its own.

### `one-day-move`: a day off, not a swap

The desk noted these as one-day moves (`desk-notes.json`) and files them under `parser` by tenant, where
they are counted. They are still a different problem, so they have their own ticket.
Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday and one in Toronto gets Monday
for the same meeting; an invite arrives before the booking. That points at time-zone handling, not date
order. One reporter says they have stopped reporting these, so the real number is likely higher.

## What to change on the desk

- Do not close #4471 on the parser fix. Close each ticket on its own fix.
- When intake tag and tenant disagree, trust the tenant: the cause depends on the tenant's locale and form.
- Matching on subject merges different bugs that look the same to the reporter. Before marking a big
  ticket known, split it by cause.

